# It works. So why is anyone still saying no?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | a clear, warm university lecturer: measured, engaged, never theatrical","b":"gemini:Charon | a clear, warm university lecturer: measured, engaged, never theatrical"}
next: {"title":"The patients you never meet: opportunity cost as the hidden loser","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/health-technology-assessment-deciding/the-patients-you-never-meet-opportunity.cast"}

## It works
level: basic
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Sham procedure
      - Nusinersen
    values:
      - 0
      - 51
    value_labels: true
    y_label: Babies reaching motor milestones (%)
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 100
    bar_colors: each
sources:
    - id: endear
      title: Nusinersen versus Sham Control in Infantile-Onset Spinal Muscular Atrophy (ENDEAR)
      authors: Finkel RS, Mercuri E, Darras BT, et al.
      year: 2017
      finding: Motor-milestone response in 37 of 73 infants on nusinersen (51%) vs 0 of 37 on sham; hazard ratio for death 0.37.
      doi: 10.1056/NEJMoa1702752

    cards vote options [{"text":"Yes — it works","goto":"said_yes"},{"text":"Depends on the price","goto":"said_depends"}] then after_vote hidden true
    icon baby center size 230 set twemoji icon_look picture of baby hidden true
    row route gap 40 hidden true
    box r1 "Effect" width 220 height 110 font_size 34 in route hidden true
    box r2 "Price" width 220 height 110 font_size 34 in route hidden true
    box r3 "The line" width 220 height 110 font_size 34 in route hidden true

    card title "It works. Why say no?"

This lecture asks how a health system can say no to a drug that plainly works.
    draw baby

    pause 0.3

The drug is nusinersen, sold as Spinraza, for spinal muscular atrophy — a genetic disease that, in its infant form, kills most babies or leaves them on a ventilator before they turn two.
    highlight baby

    erase baby

In the trial that settled it, babies got either the drug or a sham procedure, and doctors watched for motor milestones: holding the head up, sitting, standing.
    draw axes

In the sham group, not one baby of thirty-seven reached them.
    draw bar_1

    pause 0.3

    ask "Drag the bar to your guess." on bar_2 store g default "20"
        intro: Of the treated babies, how many do you think did?
        right: Close — you said {g}, and it was {g.true} percent.
        wrong: You said {g}; it was {g.true} percent.

Thirty-seven of seventy-three — about half; and fewer of the treated babies died.
    highlight bar_2

    pause 0.5

So the effect is real, and nobody serious denied it.

Yet in 2017 Norway's decision forum turned it down, more than once; and in 2018 England's NICE did too, in its draft guidance.
    text nos "Turned down: Norway 2017 · England 2018 (draft)" at.percent [62,80] font_size 24

    draw vote

    ask "Should a health system pay for it? Tap one." on vote stage own

@said_yes

    erase vote

If you said yes, most people do, and the trial gives every reason to — but hold that answer: nothing on this chart is a price.

@said_depends

    erase vote

If you said it depends, that instinct is the whole subject: everything on this chart is effect, and nothing is price.

@after_vote

The question a payer asks has two halves: what a treatmen (@draw r1@)t does, and what it costs compa (@draw r2@)red with what else the money could do.
    erase axes bar_1 bar_2 nos

So the route is three steps: the effect, which we now have; then the price; then (@draw r3@) the line that joins them.

## At what price
level: basic
use: cost_effectiveness_plane
with:
    view: first
    ticks: true
    x_label: Extra health (QALYs)
    y_label: Extra cost (million kr)
    x_max: 4
    y_max: 20
    points:
      - {label: Nusinersen, effect: 3, cost: 15, emphasize: true}
      - {label: Hip replacement, effect: 1, cost: 0.1}
    quadrant_labels: false
sources:
    - id: nice2018
      title: Nusinersen for treating spinal muscular atrophy — draft guidance (TA588)
      authors: National Institute for Health and Care Excellence
      year: 2018
      finding: Not recommended in the August 2018 draft; base-case ICER about £407,600 per QALY at the list price of £75,000 per dose.

    scratch icer at.data [1.5,10] cites ["nice2018"] work ["15 million kr ÷ 3 QALYs","= 5 million kr per QALY","England: ~£400 000 per QALY"] font_size 30 hidden true
    scratch alt right work ["15 million kr buys:","1 child: 3 QALYs","150 hips: ~150 QALYs"] font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "At what price"

To hold both halves at once, put the extra health along here, in quality-adjusted life years, compared with today's care.
    draw x_axis

A quality-adjusted life year — a QALY — is one year in full health; a year in a worse state counts as less.
    draw y_axis

    pause 0.3

Round, illustrative numbers for one child, over the model's horizon: the drug adds about three of them.
    point at.data [3,0] gesture tap

At list price, the first year alone costs about five million kroner.
    point at.data [0,15] gesture tap

Over the same horizon, call it fifteen million kroner more than current care.
    draw pt_0 pt_label_0

    pause 0.5

Divide the cost by the health and you get the price of one QALY gained — the ICER.
    draw icer_box icer_line_1

Here, about five million kroner.
    draw icer_line_2

England's assessors, with their own model, landed in the same place: around four hundred thousand pounds per QALY.
    draw icer_line_3

    pause 0.5

Now ask what else fifteen million kroner could do.
    erase icer

A hip replacement costs about a hundred thousand kroner and gives, roughly, a good year of life.
    draw pt_1 pt_label_1

On this scale it hardly leaves the floor.
    highlight pt_1 effect pulse

Spent on the drug, fifteen million kroner buys three good years, for one child.
    draw alt_box alt_line_1 alt_line_2

Spent on hips, it buys around a hundred and fifty of them: a hundred and fifty good years.
    draw alt_line_3

    pause 0.5

That is opportunity cost: in a fixed budget, a yes here is a no somewhere else — and the people on the other side are never named in the paper.
    highlight alt_line_3 effect underline

## The line
level: basic
use: cost_effectiveness_plane
with:
    view: first
    ticks: true
    x_label: Extra health (QALYs)
    y_label: Extra cost (million kr)
    x_max: 4
    y_max: 20
    wtp_threshold: 0.825
    threshold_label: 825 000 kr per QALY
    points:
      - {label: Nusinersen, effect: 3, cost: 15, emphasize: true}
      - {label: Hip replacement, effect: 1, cost: 0.1}
    quadrant_labels: false
sources:
    - id: bf2018
      title: 'Beslutningsforum for nye metoder, 12 February 2018: nusinersen (Spinraza) for spinal muscular atrophy — case papers'
      authors: Regional health authorities, Norway
      year: 2018
      finding: 'Earlier rejections (last case 109/2017: price far too high); new confidential price offer, start and stop criteria; the Medicines Agency judged that for SMA type I the cost per QALY would exceed 1 million kr even if the drug were free, because of care costs in the years gained.'
    - id: nice2019
      title: Nusinersen for treating spinal muscular atrophy (TA588)
      authors: National Institute for Health and Care Excellence
      year: 2019
      finding: Recommended in July 2019 within a managed access agreement.

    scratch nmb at.data [1.5,10] work ["Net benefit = threshold × health − cost","0.825 × 3 − 15","≈ 2.5 − 15 = −12.5 million kr"] font_size 30 hidden true
    scratch free at.data [1.5,10] cites ["bf2018"] work ["If the drug cost nothing:","still > 1 million kr per QALY"] font_size 30 hidden true
    arrow move dashed #2f6b8f from.data [3,14] to.data [3.6,4] hidden true
    text q "? new price: secret" #2f6b8f at.data [2.75,4.6] cites ["bf2018","nice2019"] font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "The line"

A dot alone can't say yes or no; it needs a line — roughly what a QALY costs elsewhere in the system, and so the most worth paying for one.
    draw x_axis y_axis pt_0 pt_label_0 pt_1 pt_label_1

    pause 0.3

Where to draw it is a choice: England's NICE usually draws it at twenty to thirty thousand pounds; Norway lets it rise with severity, up to about 825,000 kroner for the gravest illnesses.
    draw wtp_line wtp_label

At three QALYs, that line allows about two and a half million kroner; the drug asks fifteen.
    point at.data [3,2.5] gesture tap

The hip replacement sits below it: more health than the money could buy elsewhere.
    highlight pt_1

    pause 0.5

Put as one number: the threshold times the health gained, minus the extra cost — the net benefit.
    draw nmb_box nmb_line_1

Two and a half minus fifteen: minus twelve and a half million kroner.
    draw nmb_line_2 nmb_line_3

Negative: paying it would give up more health elsewhere — those hip years and others like them — than it buys.

    pause 0.5

    erase nmb

Then, in 2018, Norway said yes — for children, under strict rules for starting and stopping, after a new and confidential price; England followed in 2019 with a managed access deal.
    text yes "Yes: Norway 2018 · England 2019" at.data [1.6,17.5] cites ["bf2018","nice2019"] font_size 24

A lower price moves the dot down; treating those who gain the most moves it right.
    draw move

Exactly where it landed is secret, because the price is.
    draw q

    pause 0.5

The trial did not change. The dot did.
    highlight pt_0

    quiz "A cancer drug adds half a QALY for 600 000 kr more. The line is 825 000 kr per QALY. Where does it sit?" intro "One to try."
        right: Above: at that line half a QALY is worth about 410,000 kroner, and the drug asks 600,000.
        wrong: First work out what half a QALY is worth at that line.
        + Above the line
        * Below the line

And that yes leaned on more than the line — on severity and rarity too; Norway's medicines agency found that for the gravest form, the cost per QALY would pass a million kroner even if the drug were free, because the years it adds bring years of care.
    draw free_box free_line_1 free_line_2

A QALY to count the health, an ICER to price it, a line to judge it — and "it works" was only ever the first step.
    erase free

